<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adur Gushnasp</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adur Gushnasp was one of the three great sacred fires of Zoroastrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, associated with the warrior class and the kings. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> it was kept in the temple complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takht-e-soleyman">Takht-e Soleyman</a> in north-western Iran, where kings went on pilgrimage after their accession. The Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> sacked the sanctuary in 624.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp.jpg" alt="Adur Gushnasp" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salar Arkan - سالار ارکان · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AA%D8%AE%D8%AA_%D8%B3%D9%84%DB%8C%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%AE%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%B1_%D9%88_%D8%A2%D8%AA%D8%B4%DA%A9%D8%AF%D9%87_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
